---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import places from "../data/places.json";
---
<Base title="Places">
  <h1>Places</h1>
  <p class="lede">Six places carry this family: one parish held for two centuries, a short move of four
  kilometres that crossed a provincial border, and then the other side of the world.</p>

  {places.map(p => (
    <section class="card" style="margin:1rem 0">
      <h3 style="margin-bottom:.3rem">{p.name} <span class="small" style="font-weight:400">· {p.prov}</span>
        <span class="chip" style="float:right">{p.from} – {p.to}</span></h3>
      <p style="margin:.4rem 0 0">{p.what}</p>
    </section>
  ))}

  <h2>The Valle Caudina</h2>
  <p>Arienzo, Arpaia and Forchia lie within a few kilometres of one another along the valley road that runs
  east from the plain of Naples towards Benevento. The provincial boundary between Caserta and Benevento
  cuts across it. A family moving from Arienzo to Arpaia moved almost no distance at all — but it changed
  which archive holds its records, which is why the paper trail appears to break in 1913 when it does not.</p>
  <p><strong>Forchia</strong> takes its name from the <em>Forche Caudine</em>, the Caudine Forks, where in
  321 BC a Roman consular army was trapped by the Samnites and made to pass under the yoke. It is the
  most famous humiliation in the early Republic, and it happened in the fields where this family later
  farmed.</p>

  <h2>Why the records split</h2>
  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Place</th><th>Province</th><th>Where the records are</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Arienzo, San Felice a Cancello</td><td>Caserta</td>
        <td>Tribunal copies at Santa Maria Capua Vetere, 1866–1929; State Archive series 1809–1866; and the parish books of Sant'Andrea</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Arpaia, Forchia, Benevento</td><td>Benevento</td>
        <td>Archivio di Stato di Benevento, 1810–1942; and the Archdiocese of Benevento church records, 1575–1908</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
</Base>
